window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][135] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design, at PM level, a service that helps users find lost devices without exposing their location to others.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Devices send their location to a server and the owner looks it up.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Raises privacy, offline devices, and the stalking risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs so that even the service cannot read locations, covers abuse cases in depth (including an abusive owner), estimates load, states what privacy costs, and plans a staged rollout.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is an Illustrative design at product depth; nothing here describes any real company's system. The job: an owner finds a lost device on a map, even when the device is offline, and no one else, including the company, learns where it is. <em>Requirements.</em> Owner sees the last known or current location; works when the device has no internet; no one but the owner can learn the location; low battery cost; usable by non-experts. Must not: let a stranger track a person. Non-goals: tracking people. <em>The privacy design.</em> A lost device has no connection of its own, so it relies on nearby devices of other people. The device broadcasts a short-range signal containing an identifier that changes frequently, so that it cannot be followed over time by an observer. Any nearby participating device that hears it reports the sighting, encrypted so that only the owner's other devices can decrypt it, together with its own location. The service stores and forwards encrypted reports it cannot read. Designs of this kind, using rotating identifiers and encrypted crowd-sourced reports, are publicly described by Apple and by Google <code>[V: Apple Find My &amp; Privacy page; Google Security Blog, April 2024; Google Find My Device help page]</code>. The cost to say plainly: the company cannot help a user recover a location it cannot see, and cannot investigate misuse by reading data. <em>Scale (invented).</em> 500 million devices; each reports about 20 sightings a day: 10 billion reports a day, about 116,000 a second, and about 350,000 at a peak factor of 3. At 100 bytes a report, 1 TB a day, kept for 7 days: 7 TB. Reports are small; the rate is large, so the service must accept writes cheaply, deduplicate (one report per identifier per 15 minutes per reporter), and expire quickly. <em>Abuse-case pass (FW-C22-08).</em> | Who | How | Harm | Control | Residual | |---|---|---|---|---| | A stalker | Slips a small tracker into someone's bag or car | Following a person | Rotating identifiers and alerts to anyone whose phone detects an unknown tracker moving with them; an audible alert from the tracker after a period apart from its owner | Some devices cannot detect it; people without phones | | An abusive partner or owner | Uses a shared device or account to follow a partner | Coercive control | Clear sharing indicators, notifications when location is shared, easy ways to stop sharing, safety guidance | A determined abuser with account access | | A thief | Disables the device's finder | Loss of recovery | Requires owner credentials to turn it off or erase; lock the device to the account | Theft before setup | | A bad actor | Floods reports to degrade the service | Cost, noise | Rate limits per reporter, signed reports from enrolled devices | Compromised devices | <em>Failure and degradation.</em> If no nearby device has sighted the item, show the last seen time and place, not a false current position. If the owner's account is unavailable, recovery through a trusted contact or an identity check, since support cannot read locations. If the service is down, the broadcasting still works and reports queue on devices. <em>Tradeoffs (FW-C22-05).</em> Privacy against capability (no company access to locations versus no ability to help in a dispute), battery against detection delay (broadcast interval), and openness to third-party trackers against stalking risk (requiring them to meet the same alert standards). <em>Rollout and measures.</em> Launch to a small group, then widen (C17.2), with guardrails on unwanted-tracking reports, battery impact, and false alerts. Success: recovered devices per lost device, time to find, unwanted-tracking alerts handled. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple operates a network for finding lost devices and has described its privacy approach, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of privacy-focused design prompts <code>[V: Apple Find My &amp; Privacy page and privacy pages; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's design, numbers or controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design order tracking and delivery-status updates for an online store.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Show the order status from the database.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Handles events from carriers, notifications, and load at peak.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Handles duplicate and out-of-order events, a state machine, eventual consistency, graceful degradation, and partner variability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real design. The job: a customer sees where their order is and gets updates, even when carriers report late, twice or out of order. <em>Requirements.</em> Show a status and a timeline; push key updates (shipped, out for delivery, delivered, delayed); handle many carriers with different capabilities. Freshness: updates within a minute of a carrier event. Must never: tell a customer an order is delivered when it is not, or send the same alert repeatedly. <em>Scale (invented).</em> 20 million orders a day with about 8 tracking events each: 160 million events a day, about 1,850 a second, and about 11,100 at a peak factor of 6 (a holiday). Customers check tracking about 3 times per order: 60 million views a day, about 700 a second, with spikes on delivery days. <em>Sketch.</em> <em>Inbound:</em> carriers send updates by webhook, or the system polls carriers that cannot push. Each event goes to an intake service that verifies the source, assigns an identifier, and puts it on a queue. A processor applies the event to the order's tracking record. <em>State machine:</em> an order moves through states (label created, in transit, out for delivery, delivered, exception); the processor accepts only valid transitions, so a stray &quot;in transit&quot; after &quot;delivered&quot; does not move the order backward, and ignores events with an already-seen identifier. <em>Outbound:</em> the status page reads the tracking record (cached for seconds); notifications go through the notification pipeline (C22 Worked example 1) with preferences. <em>Duplicates and ordering.</em> Carriers repeat events and send them out of order. The processor deduplicates by event identifier and orders by the carrier's event time, not by arrival time, within a tolerance. Eventual consistency is fine: seconds of lag are harmless, but the status must converge to the true state. <em>Failure and degradation.</em> If a carrier's feed is down, show the last known status with its timestamp (&quot;last updated 3 hours ago&quot;) and the expected delivery window, not a blank; alert the carrier-integration team. If the queue backs up at peak, process delivered and exception events first. The page must degrade to a static status when the timeline service fails. <em>Trust risks.</em> A false &quot;delivered&quot; (a driver scans early) causes complaints; add signals (location, photo proof) where available, and a one-click &quot;not received&quot; path. <em>Measures.</em> Event-to-display delay (95th percentile under 60 seconds), share of orders with tracking updates, &quot;where is my order&quot; contacts per 1,000 orders, false-delivered reports. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of order and fulfillment design prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or design is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design how a streaming service starts playing a video quickly and keeps playing smoothly.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Stream the video from a server.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the content network, adaptive quality and buffering.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes startup time under different networks, explains starting low and ramping, covers failover and regional capacity, and sets measures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real design. The job: press play and see video within about two seconds, without stalls, on any connection. <em>Requirements.</em> Time to first frame under about 2 seconds at the 95th percentile; very few stalls; quality as high as the connection allows; works on slow networks and old devices. Must not: show an endless spinner. <em>Sketch.</em> Video is stored as many short segments (about 2 seconds each) at several quality levels. <em>Start:</em> the app asks the service to authorize playback and gets a manifest (a list of segments and quality levels); it fetches segments from a nearby server in a content delivery network, which holds popular titles close to viewers. <em>Adaptive quality:</em> the player measures its download speed and chooses the quality of each next segment, moving up when it can and down when it must. <em>The startup arithmetic.</em> A 2-second segment at 3 Mbps is 0.75 MB. On a 10 Mbps connection it downloads in 0.6 seconds; on a 2 Mbps connection, in 3.0 seconds. If instead the player starts at a lower quality of 0.75 Mbps, the first segment is about 0.19 MB and downloads in 0.75 seconds on the 2 Mbps connection. So starting low and ramping up cuts startup on slow connections from about 3 seconds to under 1, at the cost of a few seconds of lower picture quality, which most viewers prefer to waiting. <em>Capacity and placement.</em> Popular titles are copied to servers near viewers ahead of demand, predicted from what viewers watch; less popular titles are fetched on demand. Before a major release, copy the title to regions in advance and reserve capacity; stage availability by region if demand is uncertain (C22 CS-C22-02). <em>Failure and degradation.</em> If the nearest server fails or is overloaded, the player switches to another source mid-stream; if bandwidth drops, the player lowers quality rather than stall; if the authorization service is slow, a short-lived cached permission lets recent viewers start. The viewer should see at worst a reduced picture, not a stall. <em>Measures.</em> Time to first frame (median and 95th percentile), failed starts, rebuffering time per hour, average quality delivered, with guardrails on cost per hour streamed. <em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Netflix has published on its content delivery network and on resilience testing, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of playback and graceful-degradation prompts <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog on Open Connect and resilience testing; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's numbers or design.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design webhook delivery for a payments platform so that merchants never miss an event.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Send an HTTP request to the merchant when something happens.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Covers retries, signatures, and duplicates.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains at-least-once delivery, backoff and its load effects, ordering, replay and catch-up, merchant-side idempotency, and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. The job: tell a merchant's system that something happened (a payment succeeded, a refund was issued) reliably, even when the merchant's server is slow or down. <em>Requirements.</em> Every event is delivered at least once; most within seconds; no forged events; merchants can recover missed events; the platform is not overwhelmed by a merchant's outage. Non-goals: guaranteed ordering. <em>Sketch.</em> When an event occurs, the platform records it in an event log with a unique identifier, then enqueues a delivery task. A delivery worker sends the event to the merchant's registered address, signed so the merchant can verify its origin, and expects a success response within a short time (say 10 seconds). On failure or timeout, the task is retried later according to a schedule. <em>Retry schedule and the arithmetic.</em> Suppose 100 million events a day: about 1,160 a second. If 2% fail on the first attempt, that is 2 million retries needing a second attempt. A growing schedule (for example 1 minute, 5 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours, 12 hours, 24 hours: six attempts over a day) spreads the retry load. If a merchant's endpoint is down for an hour while receiving 1,160 events a second of platform traffic for it (a large merchant), about 4.2 million events accumulate; without backoff the platform would hammer a server that is already down, and with backoff and a per-merchant limit it does not. <em>Duplicates and order.</em> Because delivery is at least once, a merchant can receive the same event twice; each event has an identifier, and merchants should record processed identifiers and ignore repeats. Events can arrive out of order; the platform tells merchants to treat the event as a notification and fetch the current state from the API if order matters. <em>Replay and catch-up.</em> After a long outage, a merchant needs the events it missed: provide an API to list events from a given time and a dashboard button to resend. Mark endpoints that fail for days as disabled with an alert to the merchant. <em>Security.</em> Sign each delivery with a secret and include a timestamp so that old messages cannot be replayed by an attacker; deliver only over secure connections. <em>Failure and monitoring.</em> Merchants see delivery status and errors per event; the platform alerts on rising failure rates and on the oldest undelivered event. A slow endpoint (not down) is treated as a failure after the timeout and subject to a concurrency limit, so one slow merchant cannot tie up the workers. <em>Measures.</em> Share of events delivered on the first attempt, time to delivery (95th percentile), events outstanding after 24 hours, merchant-reported missed events (target zero). <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe's documentation describes webhooks with retries and signatures, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of developer-delivery design prompts <code>[V: Stripe API documentation; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's internals.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain the design of your notification system to the CEO in three minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A technical walk through the components.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Leads with the user outcome and the main risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the decision needed, cost and time in plain terms, explains the key tradeoff and the biggest risk with a mitigation, and offers a next step.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd lead with what matters to them and drop the boxes. &quot;<em>The outcome.</em> People get the right notification at the right time, once, on the channel they prefer, which brings them back without annoying them. Today we send some twice and some late. <em>How it works, in one picture.</em> Events from the product go into one pipeline that checks each person's preferences, removes duplicates, groups bursts, and sends by push or email through our providers. If a provider is slow, messages wait and retry; important ones, like security alerts, go first. <em>The numbers.</em> About 160 million notifications a day, with evening peaks about five times the average, around 9,000 a second. Our push provider's limits are the thing to watch at that level. <em>The main tradeoff.</em> We can send instantly, or group messages for a few seconds to cut annoyance and cost. I recommend short grouping for messages and longer for likes. <em>The biggest risk.</em> Spam and abuse: bots mass-mentioning people. We cap how many a person can receive from one sender and give users controls to mute and report. <em>Cost and time.</em> The first version takes one team about a quarter; running cost is mostly provider fees, roughly proportional to volume, and grouping lowers it. <em>What I need from you.</em> A decision to launch to 1% of users next month with guardrails on opt-outs and uninstalls, and agreement that security alerts get priority over everything else. I'll report weekly.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain in plain terms how a recommendation system decides what to show you.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It uses an algorithm that learns what you like.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes candidate generation, ranking and re-ranking, and what data it learns from.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why stages exist, what the model predicts and how that choice shapes results, mentions cold start and feedback loops, and keeps it plain.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Think of a very large store and a very small shop window. The system has millions of items and room to show twenty, so it works in steps. First it narrows the store to a few hundred plausible items, cheaply: things similar to what you liked, things from people you follow, what is popular where you are, what is new in your favorite topics. This step is about not missing good things. Then a more careful model scores each of those few hundred and puts them in order. The model has learned from past behavior: what people like you watched, finished, liked or skipped. What it predicts matters: if it predicts clicks, the order favors things that attract clicks; if it predicts &quot;watched most of it and did not regret it,&quot; the order favors something else. Then rules adjust the list: do not show ten items from one creator, remove anything unsafe or already seen, give new items a small chance. Finally the screen decides how to present them. Two things make it hard. A new user or new item has no history (the cold start), so the system leans on item attributes and popularity until it learns. And the system learns from what it showed, so it can keep showing more of the same: a feedback loop that product teams must watch.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A fraud model is 99% accurate. Is that good?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 99% is excellent.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks the base rate and uses precision and recall.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows with numbers that a useless model can match the accuracy, relates it to cost and thresholds, and says what to ask for instead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Not necessarily; accuracy alone says little when the thing you are looking for is rare. Suppose 1% of 1,000,000 transactions are fraud: 10,000 fraudulent and 990,000 legitimate. A model that labels everything legitimate is right 990,000 times: 99% accurate, and catches no fraud at all (recall 0%). Now take a useful model that catches 8,000 of the 10,000 frauds (recall 80%) and wrongly flags 8,000 legitimate transactions. Its correct decisions are the 8,000 frauds caught plus the 982,000 legitimate transactions it left alone: 990,000, so it is also 99.0% accurate. The same accuracy describes a model that does nothing and a model that catches 80% of fraud. That is why accuracy is the wrong measure here. What I would ask for instead: the base rate; precision (of what we flag, how much is fraud: here 50%) and recall (of all fraud, how much we catch: 80%); the cost of each error (a blocked good customer against a missed fraud); and performance at the threshold we plan to use, on recent data and by segment. Then I would decide whether the model is good by whether its total cost is lower than the alternatives within our review capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve the recommendations in a music app for a user who plays the same ten songs.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Recommend more songs that are similar to those ten.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Recognizes that familiarity is rewarded and proposes some exploration and a way to measure it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses whether the user wants novelty, designs a controlled exploration slot, defines satisfaction and long-term measures, considers privacy and controls, and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes any real design. The first question is whether this is a problem. A person who plays the same ten songs may be perfectly satisfied (a workout list, a comfort album). The aim is to help the people who would like more, without annoying those who would not. <em>Diagnose.</em> Which of these users are stuck (they have skipped recent suggestions, searched for new music elsewhere, or their listening has narrowed over months) and which are content? A recommender trained on plays will favor the familiar songs because plays are what it was rewarded for; that is a feedback loop. <em>Options.</em> (1) <em>Blend:</em> keep the familiar songs and add adjacent ones (same mood, tempo or artist's other work), a small step from taste, in a clearly labeled &quot;something new&quot; slot. (2) <em>An exploration budget:</em> a small share of slots, say 8%, chosen to learn. (3) <em>User control:</em> a &quot;more like this / less like this&quot; signal and a reset, which also respects preference. (4) <em>A separate discovery surface</em> so that the main screen can stay familiar for those who want that. <em>Cost.</em> Exploratory slots are accepted less often than familiar ones. Suppose (invented) a new song is played through 30% of the time against 85% for a familiar one: 8 exploratory slots per 100 lose about (0.85 − 0.30) × 8 = 4.4 completed plays per 100 slots in the short term, and yield about 2.4 accepted new songs. The product must decide that the discovery is worth the short-term cost for users who show a desire for it. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: new songs added to a library or played to completion at least twice (a satisfaction signal). Guardrails: overall skip rate and session length, &quot;not interested&quot; feedback. Long term: 90-day retention against a holdout. Segment by user type, since the effect differs. <em>Privacy.</em> Personalization uses listening history; state what is used and where it is processed, and let users clear it. <em>Test.</em> A multi-week test with a holdout, reading effects by week for novelty, and by user segment. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of media-recommendation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you measure whether homepage personalization is working, beyond clicks?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Click-through rate on recommended titles.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses plays and completion and retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains why clicks mislead, defines qualified plays, a layered stack with short and long term and diversity, plans holdouts, and notes novelty and offline gates.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real measurement. Clicks measure curiosity about a thumbnail, not whether the title was worth watching. A better artwork or a catchy title can raise clicks and lower satisfaction. <em>Suppose</em> (invented) a change raises clicks on recommended titles by 5%, and 40% of the plays it adds are abandoned within two minutes. The extra plays are worth little. So I'd define a <em>qualified play</em>: a play that reaches a set point, for example 70% of an episode or a second episode. If qualified plays rose only 0.5%, the real gain is a tenth of the click gain. <em>The stack (FW-C23-05).</em> Immediate: play starts and the time to first play (how quickly a member finds something). Satisfaction: qualified plays, completions, ratings or thumbs where given, and the share of members who say they found something to watch. Long term: retention and return frequency at 28 and 90 days, which is where the value of recommendations shows. Diversity and discovery: the variety of titles and genres watched, the share of plays of titles the member has not seen the like of before, and exposure for newer titles. Guardrails: search use (a sign that recommendations failed), complaints, cost. <em>Experiments.</em> Offline accuracy is a gate; the judge is an online test with a holdout, run for weeks to let novelty fade, with a long-term holdout for retention. Analyze by member segment (new members, heavy viewers, kids' profiles). <em>What I'd watch for.</em> A rise in clicks with a rise in search (members are not finding what they want on the homepage), and concentration (most plays from a few titles). <em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of personalization and measurement prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Search results got worse for a class of queries. How do you find out why and what to do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Retrain the ranking model.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by query type and checks stages.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses raters and behavior together, shows how an overall average can hide a class problem, locates the stage, handles language and freshness, and plans a fix with a test and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. First, confirm and size the problem: which queries, how much worse, since when. <em>Why the average hides it.</em> Suppose (invented) a class, say recipe queries, is 6% of traffic, and precision at 5 for the class fell from 0.72 to 0.64, a drop of 0.08. The overall figure moves by 0.06 × 0.08 = 0.0048, from 0.800 to 0.795: invisible in the average. So I segment by query type, topic, language and device before I trust any overall number. <em>Diagnose by stage (FW-C23-04).</em> <em>Understanding:</em> is the system misreading these queries (spelling, synonyms, a language or script issue)? <em>Retrieval:</em> are good results absent from the candidates (an index or freshness problem)? <em>Ranking:</em> are good results retrieved but ranked low (a new signal or a model change)? <em>Presentation:</em> did a layout change bury results? Compare before and after on a rated sample, and look at what changed on the date the quality fell (a ranking update, an index change, a new data source). <em>Use two kinds of evidence.</em> Human raters judging results on a 0-to-3 scale with NDCG (position-aware) show quality; behavior (clicks, reformulations, abandonment) shows intent. If raters say results are worse and reformulations rose, the problem is real. If only clicks fell, a layout change may explain it. <em>Fix.</em> Depending on the stage: repair the index or freshness; restore a signal that the update dropped, or add a feature for the class; adjust the ranking for that class; then test. Roll back the change if it clearly caused the drop (C17). <em>Monitor.</em> Add the class to the standing quality dashboard so that the next regression is seen early, and add raters' test queries for it. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of search quality prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A new feed ranking raises time spent by 5%, but users say they feel worse. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship it; time spent is up,&quot; or &quot;kill it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats the survey as a guardrail and investigates the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes valued from unvalued time, finds which content and segments drive it, changes the objective, reads novelty and retention, plans a longer test with a decision rule, and weighs creators and harm.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Time spent is an engagement measure; how people feel is a satisfaction and wellbeing measure. When they diverge, I do not average them; I find out why, because the system may be succeeding at the wrong thing (Goodhart). <em>Investigate.</em> (1) <em>Composition:</em> which content types, creators and formats account for the added time, and what do people say about those types? (2) <em>Segments:</em> who feels worse (teens, heavy users, new users)? Averages can hide a group that is harmed. (3) <em>Time shape:</em> is the +5% a novelty bump? Read effects by week; suppose weekly gains are +7%, +5%, +4% and +3.5%: still falling, so the settled effect is smaller. (4) <em>Long term:</em> retention and return frequency at 28 days against a holdout. (5) <em>Other guardrails:</em> reports of unwanted content, &quot;not interested&quot; taps, complaints. <em>Valued time.</em> Suppose (invented) the survey shows a 1.5-point drop in &quot;I enjoyed my feed&quot; and 60% of the extra time comes from content types with the lowest enjoyment. Then &quot;valued time&quot; (time weighted by a satisfaction signal) may be flat or down even though raw time is up. <em>Options.</em> (A) Ship, accepting the cost. (B) Change the objective: combine the engagement prediction with a prediction of &quot;valued&quot; (from surveys or &quot;not interested&quot; signals) and a penalty for low-quality content, then retest. (C) Ship only to segments where satisfaction holds. (D) Roll back. <em>Decision.</em> B, with a six-week test and a long-term holdout, and a rule written in advance: launch if the survey score is no worse than −0.3, retention's lower bound is above the margin and reports do not rise beyond +3%. Do not ship the +5% while the survey and the novelty read are open. <em>Creators and harm.</em> A ranking that favors certain formats changes what creators make; check exposure for different creators, and escalate to policy and safety if the content type is harmful. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of feed and engagement-versus-wellbeing prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you improve recommendations for new products with no history, when paid placements share the page?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Recommend popular products.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses content-based signals and exploration for cold start.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sizes the exploration budget, separates organic from sponsored placement, designs evaluation and guardrails, addresses seller fairness and trust, and avoids feedback loops.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. A recommender trained on purchases and clicks rarely shows new products, because they have no data; so they never get data. <em>Use what a new product does have.</em> Its attributes (category, brand, price, images, description), the seller's history, and similarity to products shoppers already like. These content signals let the ranking guess before behavior exists. <em>Add an exploration budget.</em> Show each new product to enough shoppers to estimate its click rate: about 1,000 impressions give a 95% interval of about ±1.35 points at a 5% rate. With 5,000 new products a day that is 5,000,000 impressions, which is 0.25% of 2 billion daily impressions. If new products click at 3% against 6% for established ones, the cost is about 150,000 clicks a day, 0.125% of all clicks. Cheap relative to a catalog that never changes. Spread the exposure across shoppers and slots, stop showing an item once the estimate is clear, and weight toward items with strong content signals. <em>Sponsored placements.</em> Paid placements mean a seller can buy exposure. Keep organic recommendations and paid placements distinct and clearly labeled, and make sure that the organic model is not trained on clicks that were driven by paid position (position bias and exposure bias). Otherwise paid products will look better than they are and the system will learn to favor them. <em>Evaluation.</em> Online test with a holdout; measure purchases and revenue per session, return rates and satisfaction, new-product success (the share that reach a minimum sales level), and seller diversity. Guardrails: complaints, returns, and the share of the page that is paid. <em>Fairness and trust.</em> New sellers need a chance; shoppers need to trust that recommendations are not only the highest bidder's. Publish clear rules and monitor concentration. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of recommendation and marketplace prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the fairness checks for a system that recommends jobs to people.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Remove gender and race from the data.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Measures outcomes by group and considers proxies.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines exposure and selection measures, tests significance, considers feedback loops and the difference between measuring and deciding, involves legal and policy, and sets monitoring and remedies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Removing sensitive attributes from the data does not remove bias, because other features (location, schools, past job titles, even names or the time of activity) can stand in for them, and because the data itself reflects past outcomes. So the check must measure outcomes. <em>What to measure.</em> (1) <em>Exposure:</em> how often qualified people in each group are shown a given type of job, especially higher-paying ones. (2) <em>Outcomes:</em> application and response rates by group, for equally qualified users. (3) <em>Error rates:</em> whether the model's mistakes (qualified people not shown a job) are larger for some groups. Group definitions need care and legal guidance, and in many places the system may not hold the sensitive attribute, so audits may use opt-in data or a trusted third party. <em>An example check.</em> Suppose among 10,000 equally qualified users in each of two groups, a job type is shown to 30% of group A and 21% of group B. The ratio of rates is 0.21 ÷ 0.30 = 0.70. A commonly used rule of thumb in United States employment settings treats a ratio below 0.8 as a sign of possible adverse impact <code>[V: Uniform Guidelines on Employee Selection Procedures (1978), 29 CFR 1607: the four-fifths rule is a rule of thumb and not determinative]</code>. The standard error of the difference is about 0.6 points, so the 9-point gap is about 15 standard errors: not chance. That triggers an investigation, not an automatic verdict. <em>Find the cause.</em> Is it the candidate stage (the job never enters the candidate set for group B), the ranking (features that proxy for group), the data (past hires), or the interface? Check the feedback loop: if users click what they are shown and the model learns from clicks, an early gap can grow. <em>Remedies.</em> Re-examine features that act as proxies; balance exposure by adding candidates or re-ranking to meet an agreed floor; improve labels (use qualification signals, not past hiring alone); give users controls and transparency about why a job is shown. <em>Governance.</em> Fairness definitions can conflict (equal exposure against equal accuracy), and choosing among them is a policy decision with legal, ethical and business input, not an engineering default. Document the choice, the tests, the thresholds for action, and the monitoring cadence. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of recommendation and responsible-AI prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or practice is claimed; the 0.8 ratio is a widely cited rule of thumb and the law varies by jurisdiction.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C23 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the decisioning for a payments fraud score: what happens at each score range?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Block payments with a high fraud score.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses zones (allow, review, block) with costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes cost by zone, finds the review break-even, measures false declines, considers delayed labels, drift and feedback, and sets monitoring and rules around the model.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. A single block-or-allow threshold wastes the middle: payments that are risky but not clearly fraud. I'd use three zones. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> 10,000,000 payments a day, 0.5% fraudulent: 50,000. Fraud costs $95 per fraudulent payment (the loss plus fees). A human review costs $1 and catches 80% of the fraud in the review zone. Blocking a legitimate payment costs $5 of margin plus some customer goodwill. <em>Zones.</em> <em>Block</em> (very high scores): 20,000 payments, 18,000 fraudulent, so 90% precision; 2,000 good payments are blocked. <em>Review</em> (middle): 150,000 payments with 25,000 fraudulent (16.7%); reviewers catch 20,000 and miss 5,000. <em>Allow</em> (low scores): 9,830,000 payments with 7,000 fraudulent. <em>Cost.</em> Fraud that gets through: (7,000 + 5,000) × $95 = $1,140,000. Review: 150,000 × $1 = $150,000. Blocked good payments: 2,000 × $5 = $10,000. Total about $1.30 million a day, against $4.75 million with no screening (50,000 × $95). <em>Where to put the review boundary.</em> Moving payments into review pays if the fraud rate in the added band exceeds the break-even: cost of review per payment ÷ (catch rate × fraud cost) = $1 ÷ (0.8 × $95) = 1.3%. If a band of 100,000 payments just below the review zone has a 3% fraud rate (3,000 frauds), reviewing it costs $100,000 and prevents 0.8 × 3,000 × $95 = $228,000: a net gain of $128,000 a day. So extend review down to the point where the fraud rate falls toward 1.3%. <em>Beyond the score.</em> Rules for known patterns (a card tested with many tiny charges), step-up verification for risky payments (ask for an extra check before blocking), and different thresholds by merchant type and amount. Measure the <em>false decline</em> rate and its effect on good customers and merchants, not just fraud caught. <em>Data realities.</em> Labels arrive late (disputes come weeks later) and only for payments that were allowed; payments that were blocked never reveal whether they were fraud. That selection bias means the model's training data excludes what it blocked; keep a small random allowance or use review outcomes to learn. Fraudsters adapt, so the model drifts: monitor score distributions and fraud rates, retrain often, and keep a human-in-the-loop for new patterns. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe offers machine-learning-based fraud screening, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of risk-scoring prompts <code>[V: Stripe Radar machine-learning fraud screening; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real system's numbers or design.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c23-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C23-10</h3>\n";
